---
// 选型旗舰：how to choose
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';

const title = 'How to Choose a Peristaltic Pump: 6 Specs That Decide the Right Pump';
const date = 'August 25, 2026';
const readTime = '13 min read';
const related = [
  { href: '/blog/yz15-vs-yz35-pump-head/', title: 'YZ15 vs YZ35 Pump Head', readTime: '9 min read' },
  { href: '/blog/peristaltic-pump-for-chemical-dosing/', title: 'Chemical Dosing Selection Guide', readTime: '12 min read' },
  { href: '/blog/peristaltic-pump-tubing-guide/', title: 'Peristaltic Pump Tubing Guide', readTime: '12 min read' },
];
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | PERIFLOW Blog`}
  description="The 6 specs that decide a peristaltic pump purchase — flow, pressure, speed, accuracy, tubing and drive — with a decision checklist and sizing example."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="bg-navy-950 text-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:py-20">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm text-slate-400" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/" class="transition hover:text-white">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-1">/</span>
        <a href="/blog/" class="transition hover:text-white">Blog</a>
        <span class="mx-1">/</span>
        <span class="text-slate-200">How to Choose a Pump</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="max-w-4xl text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">{title}</h1>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 text-sm text-slate-400">
        <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-blue text-sm font-bold text-white">RC</span>
          Ray Chan
        </span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>{date}</span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>{readTime}</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 正文 -->
  <section class="bg-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 lg:py-16">
      <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-extrabold prose-headings:text-navy-950 prose-h2:mt-12 prose-h3:mt-8 prose-a:text-brand-blue">

        <p>
          Buying a peristaltic pump looks simple — pick a pump, pick a tube, done. Then the flow is wrong, the tube fails in a week, or the drive stalls on your viscous fluid. The difference between a pump that works and a pump that frustrates is decided by six specs, and most of them are decided before you look at any price list.
        </p>

        <h2 id="flow">1. Flow Rate: Work Backwards from Your Process</h2>
        <p>
          Define the flow in ml/min at your operating speed — not the pump's maximum. Peristaltic flow is proportional to speed, so a 600 rpm head covers a wide band: a YZ15 head with 13# tubing delivers 0.007–42 ml/min, while the same head with 18# tubing reaches 2,280 ml/min. The tube size is your flow range selector; the drive is your fine adjustment.
        </p>

        <h2 id="pressure">2. Pressure: Know Your Back Pressure</h2>
        <p>
          Peristaltic pumps are low-pressure machines: typically 1–2 bar continuous (up to ~3 bar with reinforced tubing). The tube is the pressure boundary — higher pressure shortens tube life and reduces accuracy. If your process needs more than about 5–7 bar, a peristaltic pump is the wrong technology.
        </p>

        <h2 id="speed">3. Speed Range and Control</h2>
        <p>
          A 0.1–600 rpm stepper drive gives 6,000:1 turndown — enough for both micro-dosing and fast transfer on one pump. For dosing accuracy, speed control resolution and drift matter more than top speed. If you need ±1% repeatability, buy a stepper drive, not a DC motor.
        </p>

        <h2 id="tube">4. Tubing: The Only Wetted Part</h2>
        <p>
          The tube decides chemical compatibility, accuracy, life and running cost. Match the material to the fluid (silicone for general/food, EPDM for acids and hypochlorite, PTFE-lined for solvents, Viton for oils) and the size to the flow. Our <a href="/blog/peristaltic-pump-tubing-guide/">tubing guide</a> has the full chart.
        </p>

        <h2 id="accuracy">5. Accuracy: What Repeatability Do You Actually Need?</h2>
        <p>
          Peristaltic pumps are repeatable but not inherently precise: expect ±1–3% repeatability with a good drive and fresh tube, and calibrate for absolute accuracy. For critical dosing, keep a spare tube — accuracy degrades as the tube wears, not as the pump ages.
        </p>

        <h2 id="head">6. Head: Match It to the Job</h2>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Head family</th><th>Flow</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>YZ15</td><td>0.007 – 2,280 ml/min</td><td>Dosing, sampling, lab, multi-channel</td></tr>
            <tr><td>YZ25</td><td>up to 1,740 ml/min</td><td>Medium transfer, thicker tubes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>YZ35</td><td>up to 12,000 ml/min</td><td>Bulk transfer, sludge, filling</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2 id="checklist">The 6-Point Checklist</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Required flow at operating speed (ml/min)</li>
          <li>Back pressure (bar) and fluid temperature</li>
          <li>Chemical compatibility (tube material)</li>
          <li>Accuracy / repeatability requirement</li>
          <li>Duty cycle (continuous vs intermittent, dry-run risk)</li>
          <li>Drive features (stepper, IP rating, speed control, reverse)</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
        <h3>How do I calculate the flow I need?</h3>
        <p>For dosing: dose volume per hour divided by minutes of run time gives ml/min. For transfer: batch volume divided by target transfer time. Add 20% margin for tube wear and calibration drift.</p>
        <h3>Which is better — a complete pump or a head + drive?</h3>
        <p>Complete pumps are pre-matched and calibrated. Head + drive is the right choice for OEM, multi-head rigs and when you already have a drive. Both ship factory-direct from PERIFLOW.</p>
        <h3>What is the real cost of ownership?</h3>
        <p>Three parts: pump price, tubing consumption (the recurring cost) and maintenance labor. A cheaper pump with expensive tubes and short life costs more in year two — compare the tube price per hour of life, not the sticker price.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- Related 互链 -->
      <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-8">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-blue">Related Reading</p>
        <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
          {related.map((r) => (
            <a href={r.href} class="group block rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 transition hover:border-brand-blue">
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold leading-snug text-navy-950 transition group-hover:text-brand-blue">{r.title}</p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-400">{r.readTime}</p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
          Explore our <a href="/products/pump-heads/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">pump heads</a> and <a href="/products/tubing/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">pump tubing</a> for factory-direct pricing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="Spec Your Pump in One Message?"
    subtitle="Flow, fluid, pressure and duty cycle — send it and get a complete head + drive + tubing recommendation within 24 hours."
  />
</Layout>
